WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
<v Instructor>So some additional knowledge</v>

2
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.820
regarding the API Gateway.

3
00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.520
So first of all, if you wanted to make the API available

4
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.990
as an offering to customers

5
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.540
you can use a Usage Plan and API Keys.

6
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.630
So Usage Plan defines who can access one

7
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.911
or more deployed API stages and methods,

8
00:00:14.911 --> 00:00:17.340
and how much and how fast they can access them.

9
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.710
So you define what's called a Usage Plan.

10
00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.370
Then this leverages API keys to identify API clients.

11
00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.530
And then, thanks to this, API keys is going to

12
00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.590
be able to meter and to measure access.

13
00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.840
You can also configure throttling limits and

14
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.680
quota limits that will be enforced on individual clients.

15
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.266
So talking about these API keys,

16
00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:39.270
they're alpha numeric strings that are secret

17
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.800
and you distribute them to customers

18
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.780
and that's how they get identified into your API.

19
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.570
For example, this is the example.

20
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.460
And you can use them with research plans

21
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.860
to control access thru APIs.

22
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.990
So you can apply throttling limits

23
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.800
at the API key level and also quota limits.

24
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.890
That is the overall number of maximum requests.

25
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.690
Also, you can have this kind of error,

26
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.030
the 429 Too Many Requests error.

27
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.892
So this time this is an account level throttling

28
00:01:05.892 --> 00:01:08.310
across all your APIs in the region.

29
00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.950
Not for specific APIs, but for all of them.

30
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.810
And so when you have this,

31
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.510
you must implement some client side logic to

32
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.490
implement a retry mechanism

33
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.223
and the exponential backup to make it work.

34
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.790
The other thing around API Gateway is WebSocket API.

35
00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.230
So WebSocket is a two-way interactive communication

36
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.060
between the user's browser and a server.

37
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.340
And the server has the possibility

38
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.014
to push information to the clients

39
00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:37.950
which enables stateful application use cases.

40
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.740
Now the WebSocket APIs are super helpful,

41
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.110
especially with real-time applications

42
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.470
such as chat applications, collaboration platforms,

43
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.560
multiplayer games, and financial trading platforms.

44
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.710
It works with different AWS Services such as Lambda

45
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.500
or DynamoDB or even HTTP endpoints.

46
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.090
And the architecture is as this,

47
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.150
so let's take an example of a chat application.

48
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.090
So the clients will connect to your API Gateway

49
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.360
through a persistent connection through the website at API.

50
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.060
And then the API Gateway is going to invoke

51
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.040
a Lambda function called onConnect.

52
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.740
Maybe they will persist the connection

53
00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.370
to the DynamoDB table.

54
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.620
Then another lender function called sendMessage

55
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.770
to send a message and persist that message into DynamoDB.

56
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.853
And the last lender function called on onDisconnect,

57
00:02:21.853 --> 00:02:24.204
which will be invoked whenever the connection

58
00:02:24.204 --> 00:02:26.040
is disconnected.

59
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.430
So how do you channel back replies to the clients?

60
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.860
Well use something called the @connections.

61
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.770
So here is your WebSocket URL.

62
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.100
And then through this you're going to get a connection ID

63
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.043
which you're going to persist in the DynamoDB table.

64
00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.950
And then for each connection,

65
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.530
you're going to get a Connection URL callback.

66
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.050
Which leverages the @connection handle.

67
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:54.050
And if you invoke that URL so /@connections/connectionid.

68
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.270
Then if your Lambda function is posting a message into this

69
00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.650
URL Callback, which is on your API Gateway,

70
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.210
then the AP go is smart it says:

71
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.960
Okay I know I should send back a message back to my clients.

72
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.160
And this is how WebSocket works for two-way communications.

73
00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.110
On the one way it goes from the client

74
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.300
to the API Gateway to Lambda functions.

75
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.303
And then on the other way,

76
00:03:16.303 --> 00:03:19.890
anything that post data into this special callback URL

77
00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.310
with the @connections will be able to send back data

78
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.990
to the desired clients.

79
00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.050
So we also have Private APIs in the API Gateway.

80
00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.180
And they can only be accessed from your VPC

81
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.410
because they're private

82
00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.680
and you need to leverage your VPC Interface Endpoints.

83
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.590
Now each endpoint can be used to access multiple

84
00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.450
private APIs if we wanted to.

85
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.290
So let's take an example.

86
00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.150
We have a private API Gateway

87
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.540
and we want to connect from within a private subnet to it.

88
00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.460
So we're going to create an Interface Endpoint

89
00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.540
in that subnet.

90
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.970
And then EC2 Instances can access

91
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.528
through the Interface Endpoint, our private API Gateway.

92
00:03:54.528 --> 00:03:58.650
We have one level of security where we can control access

93
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.450
to the Interface Endpoint using a VPC Endpoint policy.

94
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.380
But we can also define an API Gateway Resource Policy.

95
00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.140
In that case, the resource policy has attached itself

96
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.660
to the API Gateway, and this is to allow

97
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.953
or deny access to the API from selected VPCs

98
00:04:15.953 --> 00:04:19.470
and VPC Endpoints, including from cross AWS accounts.

99
00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.360
So we have two levels of security

100
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.330
and we can use the aws:SourceVpc

101
00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.030
or aws:SourceVpce

102
00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.720
for endpoint conditions in our resource policy

103
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.903
on the private API Gateway to control access to it.

104
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.820
So that's it for this lecture.

105
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.820
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

